JUDGMENT
REFUTED: besides the real manager omission, projectConversationWindows is explicitly event-derived, writes source=conversation chunks without expiresAt, and reads source events without an unexpired clause. Its stateful-fake probe produces a permanent window from five expiring events.
The production call-site sweep also finds outbox repair in mongodb-manager-sync.ts reconstructing an event without expiresAt before calling projectEventChunk. projectChunksFromEvents itself passes full stored events, but $setOnInsert cannot repair already-created immortal chunks.
Canonical chunks also receive file/session transcript writes and benchmark-only Option A/userfact evidence. These are not necessarily governed by event TTL, but the docs should distinguish them instead of claiming all chunk reads/retention broadly.
Option B session_chunks is a dedicated benchmark evidence collection whose docs contain sourceEventIds and user text but no expiresAt/TTL index. Contrary to the supplied note, maxSessionChunks caps filesystem transcript chunks written into canonical chunks by mongodb-sync.ts; it does not cap Option B session_chunks. AgentId-keyed erasure does cover both chunks and session_chunks. If chunk projections is read literally, Option B is another surviving weakness; if scoped narrowly to event projections, it is out of scope but still needs separate retention policy.
No admin endpoint was found that lists raw chunk bodies. getMemoryStats aggregates all chunks without an unexpired filter, so expired-not-yet-swept rows can transiently affect counts/coverage but do not disclose text.
Insert-only projection intentionally keeps the original text and expiry on same-path re-projection. That is safe for immutable eventId semantics, but it makes the current missing-expiry defect non-self-healing and requires a migration/backfill.

